@* How to play Grand Legions, a preview: what it adds to Chaturaji 2027, for a player rather than an engine (that is
   docs/grand/specification.md). *@

<p class="preview-note">
    Grand Legions is a <strong>preview</strong>: still being built, and its games may not replay once its rules
    change.
</p>

<h3>The idea</h3>
<p>
    Grand Legions plays by the rules of Chaturaji 2027 (four armies, two teams, dice, thrones, command, prisoners
    and ransoms), with armies drawn from the pieces of chess's whole family: Western chess, Shogi, Xiangqi,
    Janggi, Makruk, even Checkers and Tafl, and more. Each army is chosen at random for each game, within a budget, so the four differ,
    and differ from game to game; or all four alike, if the game was set up so.
</p>

<h3>The pieces</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
        Every piece belongs to a <strong>class</strong>: Leader, Heavy, Cavalry, Artillery or Infantry. Its class's
        emblem is under its symbol, and the letters beside it tell it apart from every other kind.
    </li>
    <li>
        Click any piece, yours or not, to see its card: its class and value, a diagram of where it moves and
        captures, its powers, and what holds it back.
    </li>
    <li>
        Each army's <strong>leader</strong> starts on its throne, and does what a Raja does in Chaturaji 2027:
        sitting on another army's throne commands that army; taking it scores, and more on its own throne.
    </li>
    <li>
        Some pieces have powers of their own: the Western Pawn's two-square first move and <em>en passant</em>,
        castling, the Xiangqi General's flying capture, the Janggi Generals' face-off (a flashing button while it is
        open). A Checkers piece takes by jumping, as often as it can in one move; a Tafl piece takes by
        custody, closing an enemy piece between itself and one of yours. A river, drawn in blue, keeps some pieces
        on their own side.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Your turn</h3>
<p>
    As in Chaturaji 2027: move any one piece without rolling, roll two dice, or pass. A die names a
    <strong>class</strong>, not a kind: 1 Artillery, 2 Cavalry, 3 or 4 Heavy, 5 or 6 Infantry or a Leader. Move any
    piece of that class.
</p>

<h3>Promotion</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
        Any Infantry piece reaching the far edge may become any captured piece, as a Chaturaji Pawn does.
    </li>
    <li>
        A piece that meets its own condition (a Shogi piece in the far three rows, a Makruk Pawn in an enemy
        kingdom, a Western Pawn or a Checkers Man on the last row) may be promoted free, as its own game promotes it. Every promotion
        may be put off for as long as its condition holds.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Points</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Taking a piece scores its value, shown on its card.</li>
    <li>
        Every Chaturaji 2027 bonus counts ten times over, to keep its weight beside pieces worth far more: entering
        an enemy throne scores 10, taking a leader on its own throne 20, each prisoner sent home in a ransom you
        accept 10 (20 for a leader), and so on.
    </li>
    <li>The game ends as Chaturaji 2027 does, and points decide it, unless one team has nothing left.</li>
</ul>
